    {
      "id": "tutorial-core-concepts-what-is-a-node-graph-s2",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "How it works",
      "position": {
        "x": 930,
        "y": -200
      },
      "width": 1100,
      "height": 560,
      "data": {
        "title": "How it works",
        "body": "**Edges Preserve Relationships**\n\nAn edge tells you how two things relate. In a paragraph, relationships are easy to imply and easy to lose. In a graph, the relationship can be named directly: depends on, explains, contrasts with, leads to, references, or opens.\n\n**Graphs Are Navigable**\n\nA document expects one reading order. A graph supports many paths. You can start from the overview, drill into one branch, jump sideways to a related concept, then return without losing the larger shape.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "tutorial-core-concepts-what-is-a-node-graph"
        },
        "classRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/templates/page-template/classes/page-section.node-class.node"
      },
