    {
      "id": "tutorial-core-concepts-what-is-a-node-graph-s4",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Compare the media",
      "position": {
        "x": -270,
        "y": 460
      },
      "width": 1100,
      "height": 560,
      "data": {
        "title": "Compare the media",
        "body": "**Compare The Media**\n\nA paragraph is good at saying one thing after another.\nA whiteboard is good at rough spatial clustering.\nA chat is good at rapid exchange.\n\nA node graph is powerful when the relationships are part of the meaning. It keeps the ideas separate while preserving the structure between them.\n\n**The Graph Becomes The Artifact**\n\nThe real power shows up when the graph is not just a diagram about the work. The graph becomes the work itself: something you can open, address, revise, publish, and continue growing over time.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "tutorial-core-concepts-what-is-a-node-graph"
        },
        "classRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/templates/page-template/classes/page-section.node-class.node"
